00:02:59.337 --> 00:03:01.670
SVEN MAWSON: So I can take
that from sort of the service

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:03.410
communication aspect.

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:06.120
Michael can maybe answer
on the containerization.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:08.150
So security actually
is one of the big focus

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:11.990
areas of Istio as a whole and
sort of by extension Cloud

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:13.070
Service Mesh.

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:16.040
So we want to make it really,
really simple for people

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:18.710
to secure all of
their communication.

00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:21.128
Like, everything should
be encrypted in transit.

00:03:21.128 --> 00:03:22.670
You shouldn't have
to worry about it.

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:23.670
Everything should be safe.

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:25.210
You should limit
your blast radius,

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:28.170
like zero trust networks.

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:30.650
All of that realm of just
making security something you

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:32.025
don't have to
worry about anymore

00:03:32.025 --> 00:03:33.620
that is just taken
care of for you.

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:35.930
I think Anthos is really
going to provide that.

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:38.240
It takes care of a lot of
the sort of nitty-gritty

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:41.300
of making sure all that works,
making sure fees are rotated,

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:43.110
certs are rotated
in the right time,

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:45.680
that things revoke
if they need to be,

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.462
make it all happen the
way that it should.

00:03:48.462 --> 00:03:50.670
And then we actually give
you controls on top of that

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:53.210
so you can say, like, hey,
only this thing should

00:03:53.210 --> 00:03:54.470
be able to talk to that thing.

00:03:54.470 --> 00:03:57.320
Only this application
should talk to this service.

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:58.910
And that lets you
actually control

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:01.100
things at a much
better way than you

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:03.860
would with a typical
network firewalls, right?

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:05.600
Like, you're not
saying, this IP address

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:06.942
is connected to that IP address.

00:04:06.942 --> 00:04:08.900
You don't have to go mess
with IP tables rules.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:11.090
You just say, this can talk
to that, and that's it.

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:12.507
And that's how
Google has actually

00:04:12.507 --> 00:04:15.950
been running things for
15 plus years internally.

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:17.670
And it works
really, really well.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:20.740
And I think everyone's
going to really like that.

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:22.310
MICHAEL RUBIN: Dan
