the neat thing
about Anthos today

00:10:19.130 --> 00:10:21.560
is that you can deploy
Kubernetes across them

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:24.360
and have a consistent
interface and API

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:26.180
that will be able
to abstract out

00:10:26.180 --> 00:10:28.807
a whole bunch of the components
in these environments.

00:10:28.807 --> 00:10:30.890
I mean, that's one of the
big values of Kubernetes

00:10:30.890 --> 00:10:32.900
that you can manage your
hardware environment

00:10:32.900 --> 00:10:34.970
and your software
environment lifecycle

00:10:34.970 --> 00:10:37.340
in a consistent way with
some YAML as opposed

00:10:37.340 --> 00:10:39.977
to actually making code changes.

00:10:39.977 --> 00:10:41.810
What's also nice about
what we're doing here

00:10:41.810 --> 00:10:44.900
with Anthos is that it
allows you to-- we're

00:10:44.900 --> 00:10:49.070
providing tools to actually
do rollout configuration

00:10:49.070 --> 00:10:52.790
and other items that
are, again, consistent.

00:10:52.790 --> 00:10:55.730
Like, consistency
and choice is really

00:10:55.730 --> 00:10:57.660
some of the big
points behind Anthos.

00:10:57.660 --> 00:10:59.870
So I encourage everyone--
there's this thing we

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:01.455
haven't talked about
as much, I think,

00:11:01.455 --> 00:11:02.830
in the keynotes
and other areas--

00:11:02.830 --> 00:11:05.000
Anthos config management--
which is basically

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:07.280
how do we use Git,
leverage that in YAML

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:09.380
to allow you to handle
all these environments

00:11:09.380 --> 00:11:12.140
and resources at different
levels, keep it straight,

00:11:12.140 --> 00:11:13.910
and then deploy it selectively.

00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:15.293
So I could go and
talk about this

00:11:15.293 --> 00:11:17.210
forever because it's a
complicated and complex

00:11:17.210 --> 00:11:17.710
problem.

00:11:17.710 --> 00:11:20.180
But if you take a look
at the offering-- sorry.

00:11:20.180 --> 00:11:21.740
If you take a look
at the offering

00:11:21.740 --> 00:11:25.280
you should be able to see that
the consistency of the API

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:28.940
and the goals of making sure
that we are abstracting away

00:11:28.940 --> 00:11:30.010
multi cloud--

00:11:30.010 --> 00:11:33.140
our identity story rests
quite heavily on OIDC--

00:11:33.140 --> 00:11:36.740
as opposed to deeply
diving into per environment

00:11:36.740 --> 00:11:40.190
specific APIs is the
thrust and the theme

00:11:40.190 --> 00:11:41.800
behind the technology.

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:43.450
Does that answer your question?

00:11:43.450 --> 00:11:43.970
OK.

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:45.387
For those not
seeing it, he's kind

00:11:45.387 --> 00:11:47.780
of nodding like he
bought it, which is good.

00:11:47.780 --> 00:11:48.530
DAN CIRULI: You have
something to add?

00:11:48.530 --> 00:11:48.680
SVEN MAWSON: Yeah.

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:49.370
I was going add something.

00:11:49.370 --> 00:11:49.912
I don't know.

00:11:49.912 --> 00:11:51.328
DAN CIRULI: So
this is what I want

00:11:51.328 --> 00:11:53.360
to say is that today I
heard someone on stage

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:57.230
say with Anthos you can finally
write once and run anywhere.

00:11:57.230 --> 00:12:00.493
And any developer says, well,
I can write code and run it

00:12:00.493 --> 00:12:01.410
anywhere today, right?

00:12:01.410 --> 00:12:02.150
I can take Python.

00:12:02.150 --> 00:12:03.567
And Python runs
the same at Google

00:12:03.567 --> 00:12:06.170
as it does at Azure as
it does at IBM, right?

00:12:06.170 --> 00:12:08.840
The thing is that everything
around that running code, how

00:12:08.840 --> 00:12:11.720
you network to it,
how you authenticate,

00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:14.660
all of that is very
different at IBM

00:12:14.660 --> 00:12:17.480
and in Azure and in Google
and on your on prem.

00:12:17.480 --> 00:12:19.790
And the idea with
Anthos is making

00:12:19.790 --> 00:12:23.223
all of that consistent so
that everything around that

00:12:23.223 --> 00:12:24.890
can be the same no
matter whether you're

00:12:24.890 --> 00:12:29.570
deploying to Google or to
IBM or Azure or Amazon.

00:12:29.570 --> 00:12:32.390
And it's that
consistency that I think

00:12:32.390 --> 00:12:34.650
we're helping to give you.

00:12:34.650 --> 00:12:37.150
SVEN MAWSON: I was just going
to add that I think, actually,

00:12:37.150 --> 00:12:39.742
Anthos also can help you
with moving things around

00:12:39.742 --> 00:12:41.450
between your different
environments, too.

00:12:41.450 --> 00:12:45.300
So if you install Anthos
in all the environments

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:48.180
and it's really easy to spin up
something in a different place,

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:51.650
see how it performs, route 1%,
10%, whatever amount of traffic

00:12:51.650 --> 00:12:54.500
to it, compare things, do
all that kind of stuff,

00:12:54.500 --> 00:12:58.490
and you can actually
seamlessly move things around

00:12:58.490 --> 00:13:01.130
between clouds, between
Kubernetes and VMs,

00:13:01.130 --> 00:13:03.650
wherever you want,
without anything else

00:13:03.650 --> 00:13:04.650
having to know about it.

00:13:04.650 --> 00:13:05.650
So that's really useful.

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:09.550
MICHAEL RUBIN: I like that one.

00:13:09.550 --> 00:13:10.270
Is there another?

00:13:10.270 --> 00:13:12.390
I mean, does anyone have
anything on their mind,

00:13:12.390 --> 00:13:14.470
you know, worse
than [INAUDIBLE]..

00:13:14.470 --> 00:13:16.300
That's fine.

00:13:16.300 --> 00:13:18.108
I know Robin's got more, but--

00:13:18.108 --> 00:13:18.900
I'm sorry, you sir?

00:13:22.248 --> 00:13:23.040
AUDIENCE: Hi there.

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:25.560
I just wondered if there was
any particular CD component

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:26.805
that you're proposing.

00:13:26.805 --> 00:13:28.680
MICHAEL RUBIN: Can you
get closer to the mic?

00:13:28.680 --> 00:13:28.935
I'm sorry.

00:13:28.935 --> 00:13:29.852
AUDIENCE: Yeah, sorry.

00:13:29.852 --> 00:13:30.440
Apologies.

00:13:30.440 --> 00:13:33.050
Just wondered if there's
a particular CD tool

00:13:33.050 --> 00:13:34.450
you're proposing in Anthos.

00:13:34.450 --> 00:13:36.350
Is it Spinnaker, for instance?

00:13:36.350 --> 00:13:36.800
MICHAEL RUBIN: Oh, what is--

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:37.800
ROBIN BROWN: Continuous.

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:39.770
SVEN MAWSON: Is
there a CD system

00:13:39.770 --> 00:13:42.942
that's sort of built into Anthos
or proposed to use with Anthos?

00:13:42.942 --> 00:13:44.150
ROBIN BROWN: The marketplace?

00:13:44.150 --> 00:13:45.810
MICHAEL RUBIN:
Continuous deployment.

00:13:45.810 --> 00:13:48.230
So the continuous
deployment space right now

00:13:48.230 --> 00:13:52.130
is in sort of an interesting
transformational pivot point.

00:13:52.130 --> 00:13:54.710
And it looks like
for the longest time

00:13:54.710 --> 00:13:56.750
Jenkins has been what
people have been using

00:13:56.750 --> 00:13:58.310
as an automation structure.

00:13:58.310 --> 00:14:00.020
And it's kind of
like the bash script

00:14:00.020 --> 00:14:02.420
of being able to handle
sequential events.

00:14:02.420 --> 00:14:04.100
And right now as
people are moving

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:06.590
into the cloud, the
elastic nature of the cloud

00:14:06.590 --> 00:14:08.600
and the more sophisticated
needs of concurrency

00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:11.923
as we scale is creating this
really interesting opportunity,

00:14:11.923 --> 00:14:13.340
I think, for all
the technologies.

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:15.440
It's kind of fun
to watch, you know.

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:18.690
What got us all here is
not going to get us there.

00:14:18.690 --> 00:14:21.380
And so right now,
Google has not--

00:14:21.380 --> 00:14:24.350
we have our opinionated
conversations and technologies

00:14:24.350 --> 00:14:25.250
that we're offering.

00:14:25.250 --> 00:14:27.920
But at the same point, we
do what I think we do well.

00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:29.847
We're trying to make
the space successful.

00:14:29.847 --> 00:14:31.430
So there's this thing
out there that I

00:14:31.430 --> 00:14:35.030
learned about a few weeks ago
called the Continuous Delivery

00:14:35.030 --> 00:14:38.153
Foundation, which is yet
another Linux Foundation

00:14:38.153 --> 00:14:40.820
organization that is supposed to
make the open source world look

00:14:40.820 --> 00:14:43.520
good and have us
all work together.

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:46.010
And we recently donated
something called techton

00:14:46.010 --> 00:14:49.010
to it, which is based, again,
upon the same sort of things

00:14:49.010 --> 00:14:52.488
that we believe will
allow us to, like I say,

00:14:52.488 --> 00:14:54.530
take things to the next
level, allow us to scale,

00:14:54.530 --> 00:14:57.270
and allow us to have a
different API system.

00:14:57.270 --> 00:14:59.270
So that's a good
piece of technology

00:14:59.270 --> 00:15:00.380
to look at right now.

00:15:00.380 --> 00:15:03.530
But that said, Anthos
is very componentizable.

00:15:03.530 --> 00:15:05.330
So it will work with Jenkins.

00:15:05.330 --> 00:15:07.310
It will work with
other solutions.

00:15:07.310 --> 00:15:09.110
And I don't know
if anyone really

00:15:09.110 --> 00:15:11.960
has a clear winner for
the next five years

00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:15.140
of where the industry and the
conversation is going to go.

00:15:15.140 --> 00:15:18.050
But what's nice about
Anthos is you have choice.

00:15:18.050 --> 00:15:20.690
You have less risk that
when you take Anthos,

00:15:20.690 --> 00:15:24.680
these APIs are flexible enough
that you're not locked in.

00:15:24.680 --> 00:15:27.050
And to me, when I'm
building large systems that

00:15:27.050 --> 00:15:29.300
are going to last a long
time, that's what's valuable.

00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:31.100
So I can sort of hedge
my bets a little bit

00:15:31.100 --> 00:15:33.830
and see where I want to
put my cleverness beans.

00:15:33.830 --> 00:15:38.180
You're smiling, so again,
I feel like it's working.

00:15:38.180 --> 00:15:38.690
Or not.

00:15:38.690 --> 00:15:39.200
All right.

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:42.820
ROBIN BROWN: Can you talk
about how you connect

00:15:42.820 --> 00:15:44.671
to Jenkins in the marketplace.

00:15:48.337 --> 00:15:50.795
SVEN MAWSON: How do you connect
Jenkins to the marketplace.

00:15:54.680 --> 00:15:57.720
DAN CIRULI: I still
haven't heard the question.

00:15:57.720 --> 00:16:00.765
So I heard how do you connect
Jenkins to a platypus.

00:16:00.765 --> 00:16:02.100
ROBIN BROWN: Marketplace.

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:03.030
DAN CIRULI: And
I don't know if--

00:16:03.030 --> 00:16:04.447
MICHAEL RUBIN: To
the marketplace.

00:16:07.022 --> 00:16:08.980
DAN CIRULI: I don't know
if our marketplace has

00:16:08.980 --> 00:16:11.290
APIs that would allow you to do
that in an automated fashion.

00:16:11.290 --> 00:16:12.040
I'm not sure.

00:16:12.040 --> 00:16:13.240
ROBIN BROWN: It does, yeah.

00:16:13.240 --> 00:16:20.110
So we have-- we have
about 15 or 20 or so apps

00:16:20.110 --> 00:16:21.350
available in the marketplace.

00:16:21.350 --> 00:16:23.770
So Jenkins is one
of those things

00:16:23.770 --> 00:16:26.170
that we're leading with as
a premier partner that's

00:16:26.170 --> 00:16:26.670
available.

00:16:26.670 --> 00:16:29.810
And we have APIs available
through the marketplace.

00:16:29.810 --> 00:16:32.230
So if you're already
a Jenkins customer,

00:16:32.230 --> 00:16:35.610
you can continue to use Jenkins
via marketplace for Anthos.

00:16:39.690 --> 00:16:40.820
Any other live questions?

00:16:40.820 --> 00:16:43.040
Live questions?

00:16:43.040 --> 00:16:44.000
DAN CIRULI: Anyone?

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:47.060
I guess they did a really
good job in the keynote,

00:16:47.060 --> 00:16:51.530
so well that they told everybody
everything they need to know.

00:16:51.530 --> 00:16:55.440
How many of you are using
GKE in production today?

00:16:55.440 --> 00:16:57.030
Oh, I see.

00:16:57.030 --> 00:16:58.550
So it's a small
number of people.

00:16:58.550 --> 00:17:01.190
OK, how many of you are using
VMs in production today?

00:17:05.240 --> 00:17:07.579
What do the rest of you use?

00:17:07.579 --> 00:17:11.119
Are people using
Kubernetes that is not GKE?

00:17:11.119 --> 00:17:13.760
It's mostly AS 400
mainframe developers here.

00:17:15.890 --> 00:17:16.890
There's some Kubernetes.

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:19.339
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:17:19.339 --> 00:17:20.420
ROBIN BROWN: I'd say--

00:17:20.420 --> 00:17:21.480
how about this.

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:26.589
So I'm engaged with a lot of
customers across the globe.

00:17:26.589 --> 00:17:29.678
And we have kind of
like the entire stack.

00:17:29.678 --> 00:17:31.470
And we're talking about
product by product.

00:17:31.470 --> 00:17:36.560
But if you really think about
just like the one or two

00:17:36.560 --> 00:17:40.085
game changing effects of
the entire stack of Anthos,

00:17:40.085 --> 00:17:41.957
what are things that
you would highlight?

00:17:45.090 --> 00:17:46.817
SVEN MAWSON: You want me to go?

00:17:46.817 --> 00:17:49.400
ROBIN BROWN: Yeah, we can do the
boring like go down the line,

00:17:49.400 --> 00:17:50.780
but it's important.

00:17:50.780 --> 00:17:53.010
SVEN MAWSON: So actually,
I'll let you guys go.

00:17:53.010 --> 00:17:55.300
I'll think about it.

00:17:55.300 --> 00:17:57.810
DAN CIRULI: So I'll start
by talking a little bit more

00:17:57.810 --> 00:18:00.600
about that customer who
I talked with today.

00:18:00.600 --> 00:18:03.570
And they kind of related their
journey to GKE and Istio.

00:18:03.570 --> 00:18:05.190
And again, they're
not on Anthos yet,

00:18:05.190 --> 00:18:08.130
but they're already
seeing these benefits.

00:18:08.130 --> 00:18:11.070
In addition to things like
their spending has gone down,

00:18:11.070 --> 00:18:14.860
their resource consumption has
gone down, and they have MTLS,

00:18:14.860 --> 00:18:16.980
they have much better
security than they used to,

00:18:16.980 --> 00:18:20.910
the real benefit to them is
that they're 350 services.

00:18:20.910 --> 00:18:23.460
They used to do a total of
about, I think they said,

00:18:23.460 --> 00:18:25.710
400 deployments a year.

00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:29.220
Deployments were a big deal.

00:18:29.220 --> 00:18:33.810
This year right now, they're on
pace to do 15,000 deployments.

00:18:33.810 --> 00:18:36.567
And for them, the difficulty in
doing a deployment-- you know,

00:18:36.567 --> 00:18:38.400
many of these teams
were deploying quarterly

00:18:38.400 --> 00:18:40.050
in the past.

00:18:40.050 --> 00:18:42.835
Now they have people who are--
they're deploying constantly.

00:18:42.835 --> 00:18:45.210
And they said that the fiscal
year they're just finishing

00:18:45.210 --> 00:18:46.350
is $15,000.

00:18:46.350 --> 00:18:48.090
Next year they
think it will be 30.

00:18:48.090 --> 00:18:50.040
And so one of the
benefits is moving

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:53.490
to this real microservices
based environment.

00:18:53.490 --> 00:18:54.810
You can move very quickly.

00:18:54.810 --> 00:18:57.660
And for them, above all else,
that's the biggest thing

00:18:57.660 --> 00:18:59.500
they're getting out of it.

00:18:59.500 --> 00:19:02.280
SVEN MAWSON: So is it OK if
I go next, and then you go?

00:19:02.280 --> 00:19:05.010
So I was actually just
thinking back to something

00:19:05.010 --> 00:19:07.680
that Eric Brewer said
in a talk recently when

00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:08.903
he was talking about Istio.

00:19:08.903 --> 00:19:10.320
So one of the
things Istio does is

00:19:10.320 --> 00:19:13.440
it helps you decouple sort
of the development tasks

00:19:13.440 --> 00:19:14.730
from the operation tasks.

00:19:14.730 --> 00:19:16.897
It might still be the same
person doing them, right,

00:19:16.897 --> 00:19:18.990
but they're very distinct.

00:19:18.990 --> 00:19:21.930
What Anthos does for you is
take all those operation tasks

00:19:21.930 --> 00:19:23.120
and help you automate them.

00:19:23.120 --> 00:19:25.800
So you just can focus on that
development part, that sort

00:19:25.800 --> 00:19:27.780
of fun part, at least for me.

00:19:27.780 --> 00:19:30.030
You don't have to do as
much to do the operations.

00:19:30.030 --> 00:19:31.470
You don't have to
manage all the services

00:19:31.470 --> 00:19:33.010
and keep them up
and give them SLAs

00:19:33.010 --> 00:19:34.290
and all that kind of stuff.

00:19:34.290 --> 00:19:35.873
So I think that's
the big game changer

00:19:35.873 --> 00:19:38.430
for me is that decoupling
and then automation.

00:19:38.430 --> 00:19:39.980
So the more that we can--

00:19:39.980 --> 00:19:43.170
the more that we can take
away from sort of the things

00:19:43.170 --> 00:19:45.840
that you don't want to be doing
and just automate that, make

00:19:45.840 --> 00:19:48.810
it all built into the platform,
make it easy to control

00:19:48.810 --> 00:19:50.370
and let you focus
on your business

00:19:50.370 --> 00:19:51.870
and what you want
to do, the better.

00:19:51.870 --> 00:19:54.037
And that's, I think, a big
part of the Anthos story.

00:19:55.975 --> 00:19:57.350
MICHAEL RUBIN:
Businesses now are

00:19:57.350 --> 00:20:02.600
pretty much in a race
for software and software

00:20:02.600 --> 00:20:03.500
iteration.

00:20:03.500 --> 00:20:04.430
So I was surprised.

00:20:04.430 --> 00:20:06.260
I was talking to a
tire company and they

00:20:06.260 --> 00:20:08.052
said we want to be
a services company.

00:20:08.052 --> 00:20:09.260
And I'm like, you sell tires.

00:20:09.260 --> 00:20:11.870
They go, no, no, no,
we sell tire services.

00:20:11.870 --> 00:20:14.930
And what this really means to
me is they want their software

00:20:14.930 --> 00:20:17.000
to iterate and be competitive.

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:19.550
Software to me is
just imagining things.

00:20:19.550 --> 00:20:21.710
And what you want
in your company

00:20:21.710 --> 00:20:25.880
is the developers to focus on
imagining things that only are

00:20:25.880 --> 00:20:29.000
specific to your company, your
applications, and the value

00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:30.560
that you can get
out of them to focus

00:20:30.560 --> 00:20:33.920
on what your company needs
to do to be successful.

00:20:33.920 --> 00:20:37.220
If you have them also spending
a lot of their time managing

00:20:37.220 --> 00:20:40.760
and focusing and developing
the infrastructure,

00:20:40.760 --> 00:20:42.590
I find it hard to
understand how you're

00:20:42.590 --> 00:20:44.990
going to be as competitive.

00:20:44.990 --> 00:20:46.910
Someone else out there
may outsource that

00:20:46.910 --> 00:20:48.260
or have someone else do that.

00:20:48.260 --> 00:20:51.890
And this is what we're trying to
do to provide for the customer.

00:20:51.890 --> 00:20:53.600
I met this really
great person once,

00:20:53.600 --> 00:20:56.780
a customer, who said I don't
want my engineers tinkering

00:20:56.780 --> 00:20:59.720
with Kubernetes or tinkering
with all this infrastructure

00:20:59.720 --> 00:21:01.230
over and over and over again.

00:21:01.230 --> 00:21:05.210
We want them to come to you
with what needs to be better,

00:21:05.210 --> 00:21:07.250
and then work with you
to improve it while they

00:21:07.250 --> 00:21:09.982
focus on solving our problems.

00:21:09.982 --> 00:21:11.690
And I think that this
sort of partnership

00:21:11.690 --> 00:21:13.670
is really what Anthos is about.

00:21:13.670 --> 00:21:16.370
And I know it's a little cliche
and a little weird and a little

00:21:16.370 --> 00:21:18.190
abstract, but it's
really where do

00:21:18.190 --> 00:21:20.690
you want to spend the cleverness
means in your organization?

00:21:20.690 --> 00:21:22.370
Where do you want
to have your focus?

00:21:22.370 --> 00:21:24.680
And so with Anthos, if
you look at the problems,

00:21:24.680 --> 00:21:28.670
it's tackling configuration
management, cluster management,

00:21:28.670 --> 00:21:31.510
application lifecycle,
service mesh,

00:21:31.510 --> 00:21:34.730
you know, how involved do you
want to be in the open source

00:21:34.730 --> 00:21:36.665
management of these
items and how much of it

00:21:36.665 --> 00:21:41.412
do you want to leverage others
to shape and focus that work?

00:21:41.412 --> 00:21:43.120
ROBIN BROWN: And we
have a live question.

00:21:43.120 --> 00:21:44.030
Yeah.

00:21:44.030 --> 00:21:45.530
AUDIENCE: Hey.

00:21:45.530 --> 00:21:48.140
So how you configure Anthos?

00:21:48.140 --> 00:21:52.160
I mean, by definition,
you said it's YAML?

00:21:52.160 --> 00:21:55.520
And like you assume like
for a Rail configuration

00:21:55.520 --> 00:21:58.370
it would be like a huge YAML
file or something like that.

00:21:58.370 --> 00:22:01.190
I'm curious, like, probably
you have some SDK or something

00:22:01.190 --> 00:22:04.490
like that to configure it.

00:22:04.490 --> 00:22:06.990
DAN CIRULI: Well, I can talk
about Anthos config management.

00:22:06.990 --> 00:22:07.697
So Anthos has--

00:22:07.697 --> 00:22:09.530
SVEN MAWSON: Repeat the
question real quick.

00:22:09.530 --> 00:22:12.130
DAN CIRULI: So the
question was, Anthos

00:22:12.130 --> 00:22:15.090
sounds like there's a
lot of configuration.

00:22:15.090 --> 00:22:18.150
Is it a big, huge YAML file?

00:22:18.150 --> 00:22:20.340
And in reality, it's
worse than that.

00:22:20.340 --> 00:22:24.410
It's many, many huge
YAML files, in reality.

00:22:24.410 --> 00:22:26.320
I want to be clear, right?

00:22:26.320 --> 00:22:31.810
YAML is one of the great things
about Kubernetes is its API.

00:22:31.810 --> 00:22:36.050
But the primary interface
of that API is YAML.

00:22:36.050 --> 00:22:38.360
When I see very successful
implementations,

00:22:38.360 --> 00:22:42.830
though, they aren't making their
developers go write that YAML.

00:22:42.830 --> 00:22:45.080
You know, I see very
successful implementations

00:22:45.080 --> 00:22:49.280
where the CI/CD
pipeline itself can

00:22:49.280 --> 00:22:51.320
take the intent from
the developer, which

00:22:51.320 --> 00:22:55.290
might be a very simple config
file, and express that in YAML.

00:22:55.290 --> 00:22:59.450
So in some ways, I think of YAML
as the byte code of the cloud

00:22:59.450 --> 00:23:01.090
native foundation, right?

00:23:01.090 --> 00:23:02.090
No one writes byte code.

00:23:02.090 --> 00:23:04.790
You write in higher
level languages.

00:23:04.790 --> 00:23:08.420
And so it may be very well
that you have a simple config

00:23:08.420 --> 00:23:11.780
file that says, deploy
me in two regions,

00:23:11.780 --> 00:23:16.940
and deploy me with
five replicas.

00:23:16.940 --> 00:23:19.300
And what comes out the
other end is some YAML

00:23:19.300 --> 00:23:25.220
that goes into the config
management and effects

00:23:25.220 --> 00:23:26.330
that change.

00:23:26.330 --> 00:23:30.260
One thing that we can do is
we can take the Anthos config

00:23:30.260 --> 00:23:33.920
management can make
sure that configuration

00:23:33.920 --> 00:23:36.440
is consistent across
your-- if you are running

00:23:36.440 --> 00:23:39.770
multiple clusters, that it
can make sure that you have

00:23:39.770 --> 00:23:43.070
the correct policies and the
correct YAML, essentially,

00:23:43.070 --> 00:23:44.450
in the right places.

00:23:44.450 --> 00:23:49.970
So in reality, if you think
about all the different CRDs

00:23:49.970 --> 00:23:52.700
that are involved in a
Kubernetes implementation,

00:23:52.700 --> 00:23:55.770
the YAML can be daunting.

00:23:55.770 --> 00:23:58.262
But in a real
practical application,

00:23:58.262 --> 00:24:00.470
you might not need to expose
that to your developers.

00:24:00.470 --> 00:24:02.600
And that sounds
kind of outlandish,

00:24:02.600 --> 00:24:05.758
but I know customers with very
successful implementations

00:24:05.758 --> 00:24:07.550
where that's exactly
what they did was they

00:24:07.550 --> 00:24:09.803
abstracted away the
actual YAML itself.

00:24:09.803 --> 00:24:11.720
SVEN MAWSON: I do want
to add one thing there,

00:24:11.720 --> 00:24:14.180
which is that the
APIs for Anthos

00:24:14.180 --> 00:24:17.630
are just the open source
APIs for Kubernetes and Istio

00:24:17.630 --> 00:24:19.310
and the other components.

00:24:19.310 --> 00:24:21.300
There is not a
proprietary API here,

00:24:21.300 --> 00:24:24.320
which means all of your
configuration is portable.

00:24:24.320 --> 00:24:27.710
You can spin it all up if
you want to run it yourself.

00:24:27.710 --> 00:24:29.900
And all your applications
and configurations

00:24:29.900 --> 00:24:31.190
will be portable to that.

00:24:31.190 --> 00:24:33.440
So we don't have a
proprietary, you know,

00:24:33.440 --> 00:24:35.810
Google developed API that
we're using for all this.

00:24:35.810 --> 00:24:38.800
It's just the open source APIs.

00:24:38.800 --> 00:24:41.420
MICHAEL RUBIN: The product
Anthos configuration management

00:24:41.420 --> 00:24:44.900
takes longer to say than to
appreciate and probably to use.

00:24:44.900 --> 00:24:50.090
So there was a demo yesterday
at the multi cloud session.

00:24:50.090 --> 00:24:51.427
I encourage you to check it out.

00:24:51.427 --> 00:24:53.510
In the middle of that demo
of how to deploy things

00:24:53.510 --> 00:24:57.170
across multi cloud, they
had like a 90 second piece

00:24:57.170 --> 00:24:59.810
where they used Anthos
configuration management.

00:24:59.810 --> 00:25:01.560
And it was really
straightforward.

00:25:01.560 --> 00:25:03.560
It was really easy
to see how they

00:25:03.560 --> 00:25:05.690
took the various
configuration and then

00:25:05.690 --> 00:25:07.190
deployed it selectively.

00:25:07.190 --> 00:25:09.830
And honestly, I kind of think
that we're talking about it

00:25:09.830 --> 00:25:12.710
in a way that highlights
its importance.

00:25:12.710 --> 00:25:16.880
But the ease of use with what
that team put together to me

00:25:16.880 --> 00:25:17.653
was surprising.

00:25:17.653 --> 00:25:19.070
And when I finally
saw the demo, I

00:25:19.070 --> 00:25:21.750
was like, oh, well, this
isn't that big a deal at all.

00:25:21.750 --> 00:25:23.930
And so it's worth checking out.

00:25:23.930 --> 00:25:25.940
Instead of trying to have
us here convince you,

00:25:25.940 --> 00:25:27.110
take a look at that demo.

00:25:27.110 --> 00:25:28.693
I know that I talked
to the tech lead.

00:25:28.693 --> 00:25:30.680
There's going to be
more demos coming out

00:25:30.680 --> 00:25:31.983
about Anthos config management.

00:25:31.983 --> 00:25:34.400
And I think it's one of these
things that when you see it,

00:25:34.400 --> 00:25:37.520
it's a lot easier to understand
that the problem space doesn't

00:25:37.520 --> 00:25:38.948
have to be that hard.

00:25:38.948 --> 00:25:41.240
ROBIN BROWN: We have another
live question in the back.

00:25:45.658 --> 00:25:47.200
AUDIENCE: I don't
think it's working.

00:25:47.200 --> 00:25:52.930
Can you comment on just the
right path to get started?

00:25:52.930 --> 00:25:56.650
What's the right way to
train, to learn, to evaluate?

00:25:56.650 --> 00:26:00.610
I assume it's not free, so
somehow we have to take--

00:26:00.610 --> 00:26:03.250
we have to go from our
production Kubernetes

00:26:03.250 --> 00:26:06.640
environment, introduce, prove
it out, and implement it.

00:26:06.640 --> 00:26:09.178
So maybe just comment on
what that path looks like.

00:26:09.178 --> 00:26:11.470
MICHAEL RUBIN: So I'm really
excited to talk about this

00:26:11.470 --> 00:26:16.850
because I think I've had a
lot of learning in this area.

00:26:16.850 --> 00:26:19.330
It's really easy to look at
all this open source technology

00:26:19.330 --> 00:26:21.640
and get excited about
just the technology.

00:26:21.640 --> 00:26:24.970
And I think you leave out two
really important ingredients

00:26:24.970 --> 00:26:27.880
or components of the
solution-- people,

00:26:27.880 --> 00:26:31.708
and on top of that, the
problem you're trying to solve.

00:26:31.708 --> 00:26:33.250
And what I've learned
is that instead

00:26:33.250 --> 00:26:35.178
of going from the
bottom up and saying,

00:26:35.178 --> 00:26:36.220
what do my VMs look like?

00:26:36.220 --> 00:26:37.730
What does my
Kubernetes look like?

00:26:37.730 --> 00:26:39.160
What's the footprint I need?

00:26:39.160 --> 00:26:41.980
I think it helps a lot
first to understand

00:26:41.980 --> 00:26:44.050
that the people you're
trying to solve--

00:26:44.050 --> 00:26:46.360
not just your customers,
but your organization--

00:26:46.360 --> 00:26:49.150
and what the flow of that
organization is going to be.

00:26:49.150 --> 00:26:51.080
I tend to think that
what we're doing here--

00:26:51.080 --> 00:26:52.540
we have app developers.

00:26:52.540 --> 00:26:55.150
We have cluster administrators
or resource administrators.

00:26:55.150 --> 00:26:57.130
Then we have infrastructure
administrators.

00:26:57.130 --> 00:26:58.720
And understand their needs.

00:26:58.720 --> 00:27:00.700
And then understand
the needs and the shape

00:27:00.700 --> 00:27:02.140
of the applications.

00:27:02.140 --> 00:27:05.110
And I find that if you don't
do that first, if you instead

00:27:05.110 --> 00:27:08.320
focus on all the whiz
bang cool fun toys,

00:27:08.320 --> 00:27:10.570
you're going to find that
you may have built something

00:27:10.570 --> 00:27:12.370
that feels really good,
but doesn't really

00:27:12.370 --> 00:27:13.697
solve your problem.

00:27:13.697 --> 00:27:15.280
And I think once you
do that, once you

00:27:15.280 --> 00:27:18.250
know the shape of the footprint,
what success looks like,

00:27:18.250 --> 00:27:20.560
what your success metrics
look like, not just

00:27:20.560 --> 00:27:22.990
in the end solution
of what you write,

00:27:22.990 --> 00:27:26.058
but the operating lifecycle
of what you're doing,

00:27:26.058 --> 00:27:27.100
I think that helps a lot.

00:27:27.100 --> 00:27:29.440
And the last thing is,
what's nice about Anthos--

00:27:29.440 --> 00:27:30.880
and I think the
really great thing

00:27:30.880 --> 00:27:33.790
that Kubernetes
helps you with is

00:27:33.790 --> 00:27:37.390
it embeds the idea of the
lifecycle of your solution

00:27:37.390 --> 00:27:39.190
into almost all the APIs.

00:27:39.190 --> 00:27:40.930
So it's rarely just
about provisioning.

00:27:40.930 --> 00:27:42.400
It's also about
managing and then

00:27:42.400 --> 00:27:44.170
decommissioning and operating.

00:27:44.170 --> 00:27:47.928
And if you understand the
problem you're solving,

00:27:47.928 --> 00:27:50.470
the people you're dealing with,
and all the lifecycle of them

00:27:50.470 --> 00:27:52.270
first, I think
you'll start finding

00:27:52.270 --> 00:27:53.680
that the technology
pieces almost

00:27:53.680 --> 00:27:55.982
fall out of that very easily.

00:27:55.982 --> 00:27:57.190
I don't know if that's more--

00:27:57.190 --> 00:27:58.690
I can be more
specific, if you want.

00:27:58.690 --> 00:28:01.120
SVEN MAWSON: Yeah, so how do
you get started with Anthos?

00:28:01.120 --> 00:28:03.780
MICHAEL RUBIN: So if you want
to get started on Anthos--

00:28:03.780 --> 00:28:04.450
Go first.

00:28:04.450 --> 00:28:06.910
ROBIN BROWN: So
everything that Mike said

00:28:06.910 --> 00:28:09.160
you don't have to do alone.

00:28:09.160 --> 00:28:11.880
Myself-- and there are actually
quite a few of my team members

00:28:11.880 --> 00:28:13.480
in the crowd--

00:28:13.480 --> 00:28:16.360
professional services
has been teaming up

00:28:16.360 --> 00:28:20.812
with eng NPM very closely over
the last year and a half or so

00:28:20.812 --> 00:28:22.270
to really understand
the technology

00:28:22.270 --> 00:28:25.465
and frame out what are
the best practices?

00:28:25.465 --> 00:28:26.340
What are the gotchas?

00:28:26.340 --> 00:28:29.710
What are the pitfalls
going through everything

00:28:29.710 --> 00:28:31.570
from the installation
of GKE On Prem

00:28:31.570 --> 00:28:34.240
if you want to do an end
on prem installation all

00:28:34.240 --> 00:28:36.820
the way through the stack--
config management, CSM

00:28:36.820 --> 00:28:39.580
or the service mesh layer,
even looking at things

00:28:39.580 --> 00:28:41.230
like Knative and cloud run.

00:28:41.230 --> 00:28:45.970
Your entire deployment
pipeline also.

00:28:45.970 --> 00:28:49.390
So we're deeply involved
in all of those things

00:28:49.390 --> 00:28:51.400
and you don't have
to do it alone.

00:28:51.400 --> 00:28:53.050
You can partner
with us and we would

00:28:53.050 --> 00:28:56.430
be very willing to do
that journey with you.

00:28:56.430 --> 00:28:58.948
MICHAEL RUBIN: And bringing
up the Kubernetes environment

00:28:58.948 --> 00:29:01.240
has been happening a lot
faster than the customers have

00:29:01.240 --> 00:29:02.638
expected so far.

00:29:02.638 --> 00:29:04.930
So again, I don't want to
make a guarantee or a promise

00:29:04.930 --> 00:29:07.690
on stage, or even not on stage.

00:29:07.690 --> 00:29:10.840
But so far, the customers
have been very, very happy

00:29:10.840 --> 00:29:12.400
with how long it
takes from going

00:29:12.400 --> 00:29:15.010
from the initial environment to
actually bringing up a cluster

00:29:15.010 --> 00:29:17.590
because [INAUDIBLE] is
trained and the software

00:29:17.590 --> 00:29:19.360
has been shaped well
by people who've

00:29:19.360 --> 00:29:21.023
been doing this for a while.

00:29:21.023 --> 00:29:23.440
So getting that cluster up and
going first, maybe starting

00:29:23.440 --> 00:29:26.200
with a stateless workload,
and then working from there

00:29:26.200 --> 00:29:27.880
based on your
requirements is probably

00:29:27.880 --> 00:29:29.110
a good way to get started.

00:29:29.110 --> 00:29:31.160
And playing with
configuration management,

00:29:31.160 --> 00:29:33.320
I really can't overstate
how you understand that

00:29:33.320 --> 00:29:35.800
and involve it in your
workflow early can be helpful.

00:29:40.510 --> 00:29:42.370
SPEAKER 1: I have
just one thing to add.

00:29:42.370 --> 00:29:44.440
So for those of you that
have a team to train,

00:29:44.440 --> 00:29:46.630
we do have a two--

00:29:46.630 --> 00:29:49.820
we have two Coursera courses
that are being made available.

00:29:49.820 --> 00:29:52.360
So our training team does
work with our technical leads

00:29:52.360 --> 00:29:55.420
to roll our
architecting with GKE

00:29:55.420 --> 00:29:57.640
for those of you that need
GKE experiences, hands

00:29:57.640 --> 00:29:58.330
on experiences.

00:29:58.330 --> 00:30:00.910
And this was have
hands-on lab and all.

00:30:00.910 --> 00:30:03.700
The second piece is
architecting hybrid with Anthos.

00:30:03.700 --> 00:30:06.280
So that will be made
available in a couple months.

00:30:06.280 --> 00:30:09.610
So you can look out for
those Coursera courses.

00:30:09.610 --> 00:30:11.140
ROBIN BROWN: I
think we have time

00:30:11.140 --> 00:30:13.450
for one last live question.

00:30:13.450 --> 00:30:18.520
Does anyone have
a live question?

00:30:18.520 --> 00:30:19.020
There we go.

00:30:19.020 --> 00:30:22.095
So it sounds like
what I've heard from--

00:30:24.730 --> 00:30:28.950
so what I've heard
in the keynote was

00:30:28.950 --> 00:30:30.840
this is a multi cloud solution.

00:30:30.840 --> 00:30:33.965
But we're purely in GCP.

00:30:33.965 --> 00:30:35.340
Is it something
that we should be

00:30:35.340 --> 00:30:38.750
looking at if we're going
through the whole decomposition

00:30:38.750 --> 00:30:39.250
effort?

00:30:39.250 --> 00:30:44.830
And what does it really give
us that we should look at?

00:30:44.830 --> 00:30:46.500
SVEN MAWSON: So
absolutely yes, right?

00:30:46.500 --> 00:30:52.080
So this is about managing
your stuff wherever it is.

00:30:52.080 --> 00:30:54.630
If it all happens to be
in GCP already, awesome.

00:30:54.630 --> 00:30:55.980
Thank you.

00:30:55.980 --> 00:30:59.040
But yeah, it's going to
give you the security.

00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:00.680
It's going to give
you the networking.

00:31:00.680 --> 00:31:03.280
You know, GKE gives you
great container management.

00:31:03.280 --> 00:31:05.840
The Config Manager
portion of Anthos

00:31:05.840 --> 00:31:10.350
is still useful if you run
anything more than one cluster.

00:31:10.350 --> 00:31:12.810
If you have VMs and
clusters, you should do that.

00:31:12.810 --> 00:31:17.150
So yeah, absolutely it's useful,
even if you're just on GCP.

00:31:19.710 --> 00:31:21.810
MICHAEL RUBIN: Our
success metric--

00:31:21.810 --> 00:31:23.160
I can say that quickly--

00:31:23.160 --> 00:31:26.500
is consistency and choice.

00:31:26.500 --> 00:31:30.360
So if we end up inadvertently
doing something that is only

00:31:30.360 --> 00:31:33.390
going to work in Cloud or
only going to work on prem,

00:31:33.390 --> 00:31:35.910
that's something that we
feel we need to evolve past.

00:31:35.910 --> 00:31:38.250
And the idea here is
that it isn't something

00:31:38.250 --> 00:31:40.990
that you should hopefully have
to worry about architecting in

00:31:40.990 --> 00:31:43.785
over time, and that's it.

00:31:47.670 --> 00:31:50.880
ROBIN BROWN: And I think
let's close this out and then

00:31:50.880 --> 00:31:54.150
before I do that, just
to note, all of us

00:31:54.150 --> 00:31:58.110
will actually be at a booth with
a bunch of other Anthos SMEs

00:31:58.110 --> 00:32:01.015
right behind this red
community corner sign.

00:32:01.015 --> 00:32:02.640
So if you have other
questions that you

00:32:02.640 --> 00:32:04.140
want to ask one
on one, or if you

00:32:04.140 --> 00:32:07.110
want to continue the engagement
with professional services

00:32:07.110 --> 00:32:10.230
or something directly with
product or engineering,

00:32:10.230 --> 00:32:11.800
please ask us there.

00:32:11.800 --> 00:32:15.300
But just for a close out,
how about one piece of advice

00:32:15.300 --> 00:32:18.540
moving forward for
all these customers.

00:32:18.540 --> 00:32:19.710
Dan, you want to start?

00:32:19.710 --> 00:32:21.600
DAN CIRULI: Well, I
would say that as you

00:32:21.600 --> 00:32:25.200
look on an implementation,
think about why you're doing it

00:32:25.200 --> 00:32:28.350
and what your metric
of success is.

00:32:28.350 --> 00:32:32.730
And if your goal is to
do more deployments--

00:32:32.730 --> 00:32:34.430
it's a great thing to measure--

00:32:34.430 --> 00:32:36.680
then make sure, as you go
through that implementation,

00:32:36.680 --> 00:32:38.850
that that's the thing
you're measuring.

00:32:38.850 --> 00:32:41.220
And you know, know what you're
trying to do going ahead.

00:32:41.220 --> 00:32:43.845
You don't want to implement the
technology just for technology.

00:32:43.845 --> 00:32:46.857
Understand what it is you're
trying to change and make sure

00:32:46.857 --> 00:32:47.940
that you're tracking that.

00:32:51.520 --> 00:32:53.780
SVEN MAWSON: I have to
think about it, too.

00:32:53.780 --> 00:32:57.820
So I think my biggest
piece of advice

00:32:57.820 --> 00:33:00.640
would be to really
think about what--

00:33:00.640 --> 00:33:02.830
it's sort of what Dan
said, but think about what

00:33:02.830 --> 00:33:04.080
you want to pay for, right?

00:33:04.080 --> 00:33:06.790
As a developer, as
a business owner,

00:33:06.790 --> 00:33:09.490
whatever role you have, what
parts do you want to pay for

00:33:09.490 --> 00:33:12.470
and what parts do you want
someone else to run for you?

00:33:12.470 --> 00:33:16.360
And I think Anthos is here for--

00:33:16.360 --> 00:33:18.070
hopefully most of
you that realize

00:33:18.070 --> 00:33:21.340
that yeah, you know, I don't
need to be an expert in running

00:33:21.340 --> 00:33:23.830
hundreds of thousands
of Kubernetes clusters

00:33:23.830 --> 00:33:26.320
or in managing encryption
keys and, you know,

00:33:26.320 --> 00:33:29.170
handling zero day exploits
and all that kind of stuff

00:33:29.170 --> 00:33:31.150
that we can handle
for you, right?

00:33:31.150 --> 00:33:33.730
So think about where
you are providing value

00:33:33.730 --> 00:33:36.530
and focus on that.

00:33:36.530 --> 00:33:38.690
MICHAEL RUBIN: This is
way too much pressure.

00:33:38.690 --> 00:33:41.860
One piece of advice--

00:33:41.860 --> 00:33:43.390
listen, watch.

00:33:43.390 --> 00:33:45.430
The community now has
gotten quite wide.

00:33:45.430 --> 00:33:49.630
There are many people that I've
noticed that I'm engaging with.

00:33:49.630 --> 00:33:53.590
Very rarely have I seen
a unique problem set.

00:33:53.590 --> 00:33:55.670
And so talk to each other.

00:33:55.670 --> 00:33:58.390
Listen to each other and
talk to us and listen to us

00:33:58.390 --> 00:34:01.570
and realize that right
now I think the world is

00:34:01.570 --> 00:34:03.280
making a large transition.

00:34:03.280 --> 00:34:06.580
And as we're moving from
one environment of on prem

00:34:06.580 --> 00:34:11.260
into cloud, again, I guess
I'm in a cliche mood today.

00:34:11.260 --> 00:34:13.130
We really are all
doing it together.

00:34:13.130 --> 00:34:14.710
And so what I like
a lot about multi

00:34:14.710 --> 00:34:19.030
cloud is that we are
figuring it out all

00:34:19.030 --> 00:34:21.280
at the same time
and the evidence

00:34:21.280 --> 00:34:25.062
and the internet and the
support is really everywhere.

00:34:25.062 --> 00:34:27.270
And so that's what we're
trying to provide in Anthos.

00:34:27.270 --> 00:34:31.810
And it's why working with open
source can be so game changing.

00:34:31.810 --> 00:34:32.590
That's it.

00:34:32.590 --> 00:34:34.739
Thank you.

00:34:34.739 --> 00:34:38.797
ROBIN BROWN: I think
just really quick--

00:34:38.797 --> 00:34:41.130
I'm not supposed to say I'm
into this, but I'm going to.

00:34:41.130 --> 00:34:43.400
I work directly with
a lot of customers.

00:34:43.400 --> 00:34:47.130
And one thing I see
is a lot of customers

00:34:47.130 --> 00:34:49.560
try to boil the ocean.

00:34:49.560 --> 00:34:50.310
Don't do that.

00:34:50.310 --> 00:34:50.909
Experiment.

00:34:50.909 --> 00:34:51.960
Iterate.

00:34:51.960 --> 00:34:55.620
Focus on maybe like a
CLE in your companies

00:34:55.620 --> 00:34:58.560
that you can make those changes
and adjustments really quickly.

00:34:58.560 --> 00:35:03.543
And then the other
thing is be vocal.

00:35:03.543 --> 00:35:05.460
This is a fraction of
the team that's building

00:35:05.460 --> 00:35:07.010
this product for all of you.

00:35:07.010 --> 00:35:10.170
If a product is not serving your
use case or your business need,

00:35:10.170 --> 00:35:12.780
be vocal with professional
services or your partners

00:35:12.780 --> 00:35:14.410
or whoever you're working with.

00:35:14.410 --> 00:35:17.665
And you can really pave the
way of what the product's going

00:35:17.665 --> 00:35:18.790
to look like in the future.

00:35:18.790 --> 00:35:20.910
So don't boil the ocean.

00:35:20.910 --> 00:35:21.540
Experiment.

00:35:21.540 --> 00:35:22.410
Iterate fast.

00:35:22.410 --> 00:35:23.820
And then be vocal.

00:35:23.820 --> 00:35:27.170
[MUSIC PLAYING]

